<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muri Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1027</span> · Also known as Muri Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muri Abbey is a former Benedictine monastery at Muri in the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, founded in 1027 by Ita of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Count Radbot as the family monastery of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Its monks helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a> Abbey in 1120. The Aargau government dissolved it in 1841 and the community moved to Gries near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>, but the Habsburg link endures: the hearts of the last Austrian emperor Charles I and Empress Zita rest in its church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muri-abbey.jpg" alt="Muri Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_muri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
